One of my first [YouTube][youtube] videos was about a 3D engine I made on my [Casio][casio] calculator. I was bored in some high school classes and started writing lots of programs on the calculator.

![The famous YouTube video](https://youtu.be/ge2o3HtHDKA?si=I5_mUEHxS4UBpbWS)

The problem with this kind of software is that it only runs on the Casio, but I wanted to run it on my computer in 2023. Then I found an [emulator][emulator]! Emulators are amazing. They are one of the best tools for preserving software, including my Casio programs. There are many emulators, and they are very popular in the [retro gaming](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PCTAVbWdS5o) community.

# Running the emulator on my computer

First, you need to download some `.g1m` files and upload them to the emulator SD card. You can find some programs in my [casio programs graveyard](https://github.com/pedroth/casio-fx-9860-graveyard/tree/main/casio). There is one issue with the [emulator][emulator]: it only runs on `Windows`, and I use `Linux`. The solution was to use [Wine][wine], which can run Windows executables on Linux (also great for software preservation). Just run `wine emulator_file.exe`.
